      },
      "worked": "Suppose the number is rational. Its decimal must terminate or eventually repeat. It does not terminate, since concatenating the positive integers produces infinitely many non-zero digits.\n\nSuppose its decimal eventually repeats a block of $m$ digits. This block contains a non-zero digit, so every $m$ consecutive digits sufficiently far along the decimal contain a non-zero digit.\n\nHowever, the concatenated integers include $10,100,1000,10000,\\ldots$. Thus, arbitrarily far along the decimal, there are runs of zeros longer than $m$. This contradicts eventual repetition. Therefore, the number is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
